<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kutrigurs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kutrigurs were a Turkic nomadic people of the Pontic steppe west of the Don in the 6th century, closely related to the Utigurs to the east. They raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> repeatedly, and in 559 their leader Zabergan reached the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> before being driven off by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>. Justinian then set the Utigurs against them, and both peoples were weakened before being absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kutrigurs were a Turkic nomadic equestrian people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> in the 6th century. The similar Utigurs lived to their east, and both may have been closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. The name appears in many forms, among them Kwrtrgr in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and several Greek spellings. It has been suggested as a metathesised form of the Turkic *Toqur-Oğur, meaning nine Oğur tribes; David Marshall Lang instead derived it from the Turkic kötrügür, meaning conspicuous or eminent.</p><p>According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, then called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, once had a single king with two sons named Utigur and Kutrigur. After their father's death the brothers shared power and gave their names to the peoples they ruled. The two peoples held the Don–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> steppe, the Kutrigurs in the western part. The Utigur ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandilch">Sandilch</a> is recorded as calling the Kutrigurs relatives who spoke the same language and shared the same dress and manners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 551 a Kutrigur army of 12,000 under several commanders, including Chinialon, came from the western side of the Maeotic lake to help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, who were at war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and with them plundered Byzantine lands. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> then used diplomacy and bribery to turn the Kutrigurs and Utigurs against each other, and the Utigurs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandilch">Sandilch</a> attacked the Kutrigurs, who suffered great losses. After a peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, 2,000 Kutrigur horsemen with their families, led by Sinnion, entered imperial service and were settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p>In the winter of 558 the remaining Kutrigur army under Zabergan crossed the frozen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and divided into three parts, one raiding south as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, one in the Thracian Chersonesus and one near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In March 559 Zabergan attacked Constantinople with forces that included 7,000 horsemen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathias">Agathias</a> and Menander Protector regarded the two peoples as a threat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, yet they ended by decimating one another. Some Kutrigur remnants were swept away by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, and in 568 the Avar khagan Bayan I ordered 10,000 so-called Kutrigur Huns to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river. The Utigurs remained on the Pontic steppe and came under the rule of the Türks. Between 630 and 635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Khan Kubrat</a> united the Onogur Bulgars with the Kutrigurs and Utigurs under one rule, a confederation that authors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kutrigurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moving west across the Pontic steppe, they subdued the <b>Kutrigurs</b> and other groups and fought the Franks on the Elbe, capturing the Frankish king Sigibert I in a campaign of the 560s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 551 and again after the <b>Kutrigur</b> raid on Constantinople in 559, Justinian persuaded their ruler Sandilch to attack the Kutrigurs, and the two peoples wore each other down in prolonged warfare.</blockquote><a href="/state/utigurs">Utigurs</a></li></ul></section>
